<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Bws.Core\Bws.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Everything in this assembly is internal on purpose - it is a program, not a library, and
      nothing outside it has any business calling in. That left it with no unit tests at all and,
      because the integration tests run the built executable as a separate process, with no
      coverage measurement either: 3135 lines the gate could not see.

      Opening it to one named test assembly is the narrow way out. The alternative - making types
      public so a test can reach them - would widen the shipped surface to buy a test, which is
      the wrong direction for a program that ships as one file.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Bws.Cli.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE FILE ON DISK IS bws.exe, AND THE PROJECT STAYS Bws.Cli. Two different names for two
    different things, and until 2026-09-22 the file borrowed the project's.

    The usage text has said `bws list`, `bws snapshot create` from the first day, every example in
    the README says it, and the person typing it at a server console is the one the name is for -
    a file called Bws.Cli.exe made every one of those lines a lie the reader had to correct by hand.
    The project name is what the tree, the namespaces, the test project and the coverage floor are
    organised around, and none of that is seen by a user.

    What this does NOT change, said because each was checked rather than assumed: the root
    namespace stays Bws.Cli (it follows the project name, not this property), the embedded resource
    keeps its explicit LogicalName below, and the integration tests look for the file by THIS name
    - tests/Bws.Integration.Tests/CommandLineTool.cs. The architecture guards read the assembly name
    out of this file rather than carrying a copy of it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>bws</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      User-facing text is embedded, not shipped next to the executable. One less way for
      the tool to arrive broken, and it survives publishing as a single native file.
    -->
    <!--
      WithCulture=false is load-bearing, not decoration.

      MSBuild reads the ".en." in the file name as a culture and, left alone, compiles the
      file into a satellite assembly under en\ instead of embedding it here, stripping the
      culture out of the resource name on the way. The build stays green and the tool dies
      on its first line saying the resource is missing.

      Our language files are plain data we load and choose between ourselves. They are not
      .NET satellite resources and must not be treated as such. The same applies to every
      gui.<language>.json that follows.

      LogicalName is spelled out for the same reason: the default is derived from the root
      namespace and the folder, and implicit rules are what failed here in the first place.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Resources\cli.en.json"
                      LogicalName="Bws.Cli.Resources.cli.en.json"
                      WithCulture="false" />

    <!--
      THE COMPONENT REGISTER, LINKED FROM packaging/ RATHER THAN COPIED INTO THIS FOLDER.

      packaging/components.json is the one source for three renderings: the SPDX document
      published beside every archive, the full licence texts in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md, and the
      licence command inside this executable. A copy here would be a second file to update and a
      first one to forget, which is exactly how a notices file goes stale, and this repository has
      the measurement to prove it happens.

      (The component switch cannot be named in this comment: XML forbids two hyphens inside one,
      and the first version of this block said the whole project file could not be loaded.)

      Embedded rather than shipped beside the program for the same reason as the text above: both
      halves publish as ONE self-contained file, and an answer about what is inside that file must
      not depend on a second file being next to it.

      LogicalName spelled out, and the folder in the name is deliberately Resources: the file does
      not live there, and a resource name derived from its real path would carry `packaging` into
      the assembly and break the moment the file moved.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\packaging\components.json"
                      LogicalName="Bws.Cli.Resources.components.json"
                      WithCulture="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Windows shows this as the file description, in properties and in Task Manager. -->
    <AssemblyTitle>Better Windows Services (command line)</AssemblyTitle>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE SAME FILE THE WINDOW USES, not a copy - see the longer note in Bws.Gui.csproj.

    A console program does have an icon worth setting: Explorer shows it, a shortcut takes it,
    and so does the taskbar while the program runs. One product, one mark.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ApplicationIcon>..\bws.ico</ApplicationIcon>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>